WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.760
You share a card with your sister.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.153
You pay 30.00 for coffee, and your app shows 70.00 left.

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:12.836
A moment later, her app shows 100.00, as if you'd never paid.

4
00:00:12.836 --> 00:00:14.375
She refreshes: 70.00.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.758
Nothing is broken.

6
00:00:15.758 --> 00:00:20.841
The company keeps each balance on three computers, so losing one loses nothing.

7
00:00:20.841 --> 00:00:26.375
Your payment reached one copy first, and her phone asked a copy that hadn't heard yet.

8
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.462
So here's the real question.

9
00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:31.920
With three copies, what is a read allowed to return?

10
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.383
Was that 100.00 a bug?

11
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:35.961
On one computer the answer is easy.

12
00:00:35.961 --> 00:00:40.419
A read returns the last value written, like a variable in a program.

13
00:00:40.419 --> 00:00:45.271
With three copies, the last value depends on which copy you ask, and when.

14
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:48.622
Picture three noticeboards in three rooms of a house.

15
00:00:48.622 --> 00:00:54.801
You write the new balance on the nearest board, and somebody carries it to the other two, sooner or later.

16
00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:57.948
A read is a glance at the board in the room you're in.

17
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.717
The boards are the copies, the walk between rooms is the delay, and the glance is a read.

18
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:06.010
What we need is a set of house rules.

19
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.436
What may a glance show?

20
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.917
To check a rule, we draw a history.

21
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:11.501
Each program gets a line.

22
00:01:11.501 --> 00:01:16.125
Each request is a bar, from when it's sent to when the answer comes back.

23
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.658
Checkout writes the balance, 70.

24
00:01:18.658 --> 00:01:21.308
The ledger, starting later, reads 70.

25
00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:23.595
The house rules form a ladder.

26
00:01:23.595 --> 00:01:29.815
Each rung down drops one demand, so a history that passes a rung passes every rung below it.

27
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.195
This history passes every rung.

28
00:01:32.195 --> 00:01:34.764
One copy could have done exactly this.

29
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:36.895
The top rung is linearizable.

30
00:01:36.895 --> 00:01:43.702
The store behaves as if there were one copy, and each operation took effect at one instant inside its bar.

31
00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:47.619
So a read that starts after a write has finished must see it.

32
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:53.074
Now the ledger starts after the write has finished, and still reads the old value.

33
00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:55.741
The dash means the value before the write.

34
00:01:55.741 --> 00:01:57.011
Which rungs survive?

35
00:01:57.011 --> 00:01:58.027
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:02.816
Only the top one breaks.

37
00:02:02.816 --> 00:02:05.191
Real time says the write came first.

38
00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:08.357
But the rung below doesn't care about real time.

39
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:13.214
That's sequential consistency, which Leslie Lamport defined in 1979.

40
00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:19.951
It keeps one order that everybody agrees on, with each program's own steps in the order it ran them.

41
00:02:19.951 --> 00:02:24.397
Put the ledger's read before the write, and nobody's order breaks.

42
00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:35.681
To break it, two copies, one in Europe and one in the United States, accept two writes to a payment's status at the same moment: 2 for captured, and 3 for refunded.

43
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:37.859
The ledger sees 2, then 3.

44
00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:40.302
The fraud checker sees 3, then 2.

45
00:02:40.302 --> 00:02:42.524
No single order explains both.

46
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:46.297
One rung down, causal consistency drops the single order.

47
00:02:46.297 --> 00:02:51.127
Two writes that couldn't have known about each other may show up in any order.

48
00:02:51.127 --> 00:02:54.285
What it keeps is: never an effect before its cause.

49
00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:57.125
A support agent asks a shop a question.

50
00:02:57.125 --> 00:02:59.119
The shop reads it and answers.

51
00:02:59.119 --> 00:03:02.575
An auditor reads the answer, then finds no question.

52
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.343
The answer was caused by the question, so causal breaks.

53
00:03:06.343 --> 00:03:10.835
It's a reply that says yes, see you there, with no invitation anywhere.

54
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:16.388
Below causal sit promises to one client about its own run of requests, its session.

55
00:03:16.388 --> 00:03:19.744
Read your writes: after you write, your reads see it.

56
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.511
Checkout writes 70, and its very next read gets the old value.

57
00:03:24.511 --> 00:03:25.603
That breaks it.

58
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:30.391
Monotonic reads: once you've seen a value, you never see an older one.

59
00:03:30.391 --> 00:03:34.721
The ledger reads 70, then the old value from a copy further behind.

60
00:03:34.721 --> 00:03:36.660
Its balance went back in time.

61
00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:39.614
The bottom rung is eventual consistency.

62
00:03:39.614 --> 00:03:42.764
If the writes stop, all copies end up the same.

63
00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:46.383
It says nothing about what a read returns before then.

64
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:49.343
That's why it held in every history we drew.

65
00:03:49.343 --> 00:03:52.931
No history that stops can break a promise with no deadline.

66
00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:57.311
It's the friend who'll pay you back eventually: never technically wrong.

67
00:03:57.567 --> 00:04:02.907
Each rung has a name: a consistency model, a rule for which histories are right.

68
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.173
So why not always take the top?

69
00:04:05.173 --> 00:04:12.045
Because each rung up costs availability: the ability to keep answering while part of the network is cut off.

70
00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:18.888
When the network splits the copies, no model at sequential or above lets every copy keep answering.

71
00:04:18.888 --> 00:04:20.019
Some must refuse.

72
00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:26.208
Causal and the session promises can keep working, if a client keeps talking to the same copy.

73
00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:29.394
A stronger model is a promise someone pays for.

74
00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:32.292
Pick the loosest rung your data can live with.

75
00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:33.917
One quick check.

76
00:04:33.917 --> 00:04:38.448
Your sister read 100.00 after you had already paid, then 70.00.

77
00:04:38.448 --> 00:04:40.389
Which rungs did that break?

78
00:04:40.389 --> 00:04:41.540
Pause and think.

79
00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:45.839
Only the top one.

80
00:04:45.839 --> 00:04:51.265
She didn't write anything, her balance never went back, and one order explains it all.

81
00:04:51.265 --> 00:04:55.240
Nothing was broken: the store just promised less than one copy.
